data.content:
Here's what happened and how to fix it:

**Root cause:** C# 14 introduces new span conversion rules. `array.Contains(num)` now binds to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. The `MemoryExtensions` overload **cannot be represented in expression trees**, so it throws at runtime (especially with `preferInterpretation: true`).

**Fix — any of these options:**

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast to IEnumerable<T>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three force the compiler to bind to `Enumerable.Contains`, which is expression-tree compatible.
